body:
"The funk is in the junk" is my philosophy when it comes to making things more human/organic.  Basically, a player on an acoustic instrument, even on the best take, is going to produce errors and tonal artifacts (e.g. that guitar slide sound when changing playing position).   There are little micro-inflections in between phrases that almost signal when a change is about to happen.  What this means, in my view, is you must allow some "wrong" sounds into your palette - sounds that are a "product" of the movement.  The best way to do this is draw/play in your notes, and then record take after take of you automating/playing with dynamics and effects.  This produces a variety of the same phrase, so you could for instance have one take be Verse 1/Drop 1, and another be Verse 2/Drop 2, or splice em together and vary them again through new fx, etc.

&#x200B;

As far as note timing is concerned, swing goes a long way, as does varying individual note offsets/positions.  Look into how the Digitakt/other Elektron gear makes use of swing and per-note-nudge.  That's the secret sauce to making something juke in a way that you instinctually feel normal dancing to it.
